goal: have an llm mimic a client in an intake setup, where if the student groups do a reasonable job doing the intake then they would guess the correct diagnosis. Maybe there would be a graduated complexity to the clients across the five weeks. 

Here is what the instructor is requesting: 

I am an instructor of classes that teach doctoral- and master’s-level clinical psychology students diagnostic skills. To arrive at a psychological diagnosis for a client, clinicians usually ask a series of questions in what is called an intake (or clinical interview). During the intake, the client will answer questions about symptoms such as what psychological symptoms they are experiencing, when the symptoms started, how long they have been experiencing the symptoms, under what circumstances they experience the symptoms, when the symptoms are better or worse, and similar information.

 

To figure out the diagnosis, the clinician will then compare the symptoms the client described to the symptoms listed for various diagnoses (or disorders) in the fifth edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-text revision (DSM-5-TR or “DSM”), which was published by the American Psychiatric Association in 2022. The DSM divides psychological diagnoses into categories such as “the depressive and related disorders,” “the bipolar and related disorders,” and “schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders,” and many others. Within each of those categories, there are several diagnoses. For example, the category of “depressive and related disorders” includes diagnoses such as major depressive disorder, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia), and disruptive mood dysregulation disorder, among others. For each diagnosis, there is a list that includes required criteria. Each criterion much be met for the clinician to say that is the correct diagnosis for the client’s presenting problem. For example, to diagnosis major depressive disorder, there are 5 criteria that must be met. To determine if a criterion is met, the criterion has to be true for the client being interviewed. In many cases, the criterion will require that a certain number of symptoms are met. For example, Criterion A of major depressive disorder lists 9 symptoms; to say that Criterion A is “met” or “satisfied,” at least 5 of the listed symptoms must be true for the client, and one of the symptoms the client is experiencing must be either the first or second symptom listed. If all 5 of the criteria for major depressive disorder are met, the clinician can make the diagnosis. If even one criterion is not met, the clinician cannot say that diagnosis fits the symptoms the client is experiencing, so the diagnosis cannot be made. Sometimes a criterion describes something the client is experiencing or has experienced-for example, “depressed mood” for major depressive disorder. Sometimes a criterion is about something the client must NOT have experienced for the diagnosis to be true-for example, to be diagnosed with major depressive disorder, a client cannot have ever had a manic or hypomanic episode, because that would indicate a different kind of diagnosis. Graduate students in psychology who are going to provide therapy to clients need to understand how to conduct intakes with clients and get the information they need to make accurate diagnoses, because knowing what diagnosis best fits with the client’s symptoms helps them know the best evidence-based treatment to use with the client.

 

These types of intakes generally last between 20 and 90 minutes; because of the time constraints of a graduate class setting, the intakes the students will do should last 30 minutes. The students will complete these intakes in a small group (around 5 to 7 students in each group) over the course of 5 weeks. The point of this exercise is for students to get to practice their interviewing and diagnostic skills as they gain more knowledge about different psychological diagnoses across the 5 weeks of the course. Most graduate students begin their training without knowing very much about how to conduct an effective intake to get the information they need to make an accurate diagnosis, but they learn more as they go through the course. So, it should be easier for the students to figure out the diagnosis based on the symptoms the client is describing in week 1, then get harder as the students get more familiar with conducting intakes and with different diagnoses over weeks 2, 3, 4, and 5. In the real world, clients do not always understand their own symptoms, and they don’t use the same words the DSM uses. For example, a client experiencing symptoms of a major depressive episode would probably not say, “I am experiencing markedly diminished interest or pleasure in all, or almost all, activities most of the day, nearly every day,” but a client might say something like “Nothing is fun anymore.” A client experiencing a manic episode would probably not say, “For the past week I have been experiencing a distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive, or irritable mood and abnormally and persistently increased activity or energy,” but they might say, “I have been on top of the world and I feel like there’s nothing I can’t do.” It is important that students learn to pay attention to timeframes, as well, because for most diagnoses the symptoms have to have lasted a certain amount of time (for example, at least 1 week for a manic episode or at least 2 weeks for a major depressive episode). Nonverbal communication is also important in diagnosis. A client who is experiencing a manic episode might talk rapidly and tangentially, whereas a client experiencing a major depressive episode might speak slowly and seem barely able to get the words out. A client experiencing certain psychotic symptoms might seem to be listening to voices other people cannot hear, and their speech might not make much sense to a listener (“word salad”). The students are being trained to ask questions that help get the information they need to make an accurate diagnosis, and to pay attention to how the client presents in the room and their tone, body language, speech patterns, etc.

 

The students study a few diagnostic categories each week. In the course, we will cover diagnoses in these main categories in this order: Week 1-depressive disorders; bipolar and related disorders; and schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders.

Week 2-feeding and eating disorders; elimination disorders; somatic symptom and related disorders.

Week 3-substance related and addictive disorders (we spend the most time on Alcohol-, Caffeine-, Cannabis-, and Tobacco-Related Disorders, and Gambling Disorder).

Week 4-anxiety disorders; obsessive-compulsive and related disorders; trauma- and stressor-related disorders; dissociative disorders (we only really study dissociative disorder); and personality disorders.

Week 5- disruptive, impulse-control, and conduct disorders; neurodevelopmental Disorders; and neurocognitive Disorders.

                             

So, for week 1, you should create 5 different clients, each of whom will describe a presenting problem that includes symptoms that meet criteria for one of the diagnoses we cover during week 1. The students will take turns in their small groups asking the client questions to try to arrive at the correct diagnosis. AFTER the 30-minute intake, ask the students what diagnosis they think fits the client’s symptoms. Then, tell them what diagnosis the client was trying to demonstrate, and point out specific information the client provided that aligns with diagnostic criteria and symptoms for that particular diagnosis. This will be a useful training tool for students to see what different kinds of questions they could have asked and what information they interpreted correctly or incorrectly. Please keep a transcript of each intake for each group so that the students, the instructor, and the TA for the course can review what happened during the intake.

 

The process will repeat each week, with the students interviewing a new client with different symptoms and a different diagnosis that aligns with the materials are studying for that week.

 

During an intake, clinicians don’t just ask about symptoms, so the clients should be able to talk about demographic and background information, as well. These include things like their name, date of birth, age, race/ethnicity, gender (including female, male, gender fluid, nonbinary, etc.), sexual orientation (including straight, gay, lesbian, bisexual, asexual, etc.), relationship status, disabilities (if any), socioeconomic status, information about where they were born and where they grew up, information about family of origin such as parents or guardians, siblings (if any), and important people in their lives (such as close friends, past and present partners, etc.), employment status (if any), education history, military service history (if any), medical history (including any major illnesses, hospitalizations, losses of consciousness, surgeries, etc.), any past or current mental health or physical diagnoses, any past or current prescription medications, any substances used or taken without a prescription (including alcohol, drugs, and over-the-counter medications or supplements); legal system involvement (if any-this is not always the case); whether they have been in therapy before and if so what kind of therapy and how it went; goals for therapy (what would “better” be like? what are they hoping to get out of therapy?); if they have ever experienced or witnesses abuse or trauma, including sexual assault, domestic violence, child abuse, or emotional abuse; if they have any suicidal ideation (some clients may have thought about killing themselves or hurting others to a limited degree, but given that these are students just learning to do diagnosis, do not have clients who have attempted suicide or actually engaged in physical violence against another person). Do not include graphic details of any type of violence, sexual assault, etc. This is a learning exercise and is not intended to be triggering to any student, particularly those who may have experienced similar events themselves. All clients should be adults over the age of 18 and able to speak and understand English and participate effectively in this process.

 

Clients also have very different personalities. Some are eager to talk; some are hesitant, embarrassed, uncomfortable, or even resistant to answering questions. Some may not speak English as a first language, and some may find it more difficult than others to explain their symptoms BECAUSE of the symptoms themselves. I want the students to be able to get enough information to make a reasonable diagnosis, but it should not be so easy that they are not developing skills and learning to work with a variety of clients. If the practice client is too helpful, students will not be prepared for clients who may be more challenging in the real world.

 

Real clients do not share a lot of information in each comment. They may say only a few words at a time, and answer slowly or incompletely (depending on the symptoms they are experiencing – a manic client would say a lot, quickly, but not someone depressed or psychotic). Most real clients would probably not respond with more than 25 words at a time. Sometimes real clients are confused by a question and ask for clarification. Sometimes they refuse to answer a question about a certain topic, particularly if it is a painful topic. The students are learning skills to help them deal with those kinds of situations, so hopefully they can rephrase a question or help the client become more comfortable when that happens.

 

I want the students to feel challenged by the process, but also to enjoy it and find it rewarding. I think they will enjoy it and find it rewarding if it is challenging enough that they believe they are really being asked to learn something and grow their clinical skills, but not SO challenging that they feel frustrated or like it is beyond their capability to work effectively with the client. Each week, each group will have a new client, so they can use the skills they learned the previous week to assist them, and by the end of the class they will have had the experience of working with 5 different clients with different personalities, histories, response styles, presenting problems, and diagnoses. This should help the students gain skills and confidence to continue their training and to work more effectively with clients in the real world.  

 

Thank you for helping me create this training opportunity for students. It means a lot to me that students develop strong skills and find their classes meaningful and useful. 